   "hint": "An identity must hold for every angle.",
   "explain": "An identity holds for every angle where both sides are defined. One angle where the sides differ is enough to show the statement is not an identity."
  }
 ],
 "steps": [
  {
   "id": "s0",
   "title": "The question",
   "text": "\\[\\begin{aligned}&\\text{Verify the identities:}\\\\[5pt]&{\\dfrac{\\sin t}{1-\\cos t}-\\dfrac{\\sin t\\cos t}{1+\\cos t}=\\csc t\\,(1+\\cos^2t)}\\end{aligned}\\]",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {},
     "at": 0
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "s1",
   "title": "Max & min",
   "text": "\\[\\begin{aligned}&\\text{LHS with common denominator}\\\\[5pt]&{(1-\\cos t)(1+\\cos t)=1-\\cos^2t=\\sin^2t}\\text{:}\\\\[5pt]&\\frac{\\sin t(1+\\cos t)-\\sin t\\cos t(1-\\cos t)}{\\sin^2t}\\\\[5pt]&{}=\\frac{\\sin t\\,[\\,1+\\cos t-\\cos t+\\cos^2t\\,]}{\\sin^2t}\\\\[5pt]&{}=\\frac{\\sin t\\,(1+\\cos^2t)}{\\sin^2t}\\\\[5pt]&{}=\\frac{1+\\cos^2t}{\\sin t}=\\csc t\\,(1+\\cos^2t).\\ \\blacksquare\\end{aligned}\\]",
   "actions": []
  }
 ],
 "takeaways": [
  "An **identity** is true for every angle: the two sides draw the same curve.",
  "The Difference readout should stay at 0 wherever the probe goes.",
  "Check one or two angles as a test, but a proof needs algebra."
 ],
